# Limitations

## No indented code

::::plate[Four spaces of indent](mark="····", tone=fence)
Indentation means nothing in mdsvex, since it is how markup inside elements and components is laid out. Four spaces of indent don't make a code block.

```svx commonmark cue="^    "
    code
```

:::notes
Use a fence. On the atlas: [code fence](/atlas#code-fence).
:::
::::

::::plate[An indented fence](mark="```", tone=fence)
A fence can be indented, inside an element or not. Its code keeps the indent it was written with:

````svx score
<div>
	```js
	let x;
	```
</div>
````

```html
<div><pre><code class="language-js">	let x;</code></pre></div>
```
::::

## Not read yet

::::plate[Bare urls](mark="https:", tone=link)
Only `<https://example.com>` is a link, unless [`@mdsvex/plugin-references`](/docs/plugins/references) is set to link them.

```svx commonmark gfm cue="https://\S+"
See https://example.com
```

:::notes
On the atlas: [autolink](/atlas#autolink).
:::
::::

- **GitHub alerts, definition lists and wiki links**, see [nothing yet](/docs/guides/remark-and-rehype#nothing-yet). `==mark==` is text too, write [`:mark[text]`](/docs/plugins/elements).

## Not possible yet

- **Replacements of its own for a component.** Markdown inside a component uses the document's replacements, see [nested scopes](/docs/guides/components#nested-scopes) for the way around it.
- **A plugin that adds syntax.** A plugin changes the tree the parser made, and can rewrite text, but what is markdown is fixed.
